research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-118-### Perturbative renormalization and anomaly control
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-119-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:120:Keep three mathematical objects separate: formal Feynman coefficients after gauge fixing; locally renormalized time-ordered products/counterterms; and a nonperturbatively defined quantum theory. A perturbative gauge-renormalization item must specify the regularization scheme, gauge parameter, ghosts, matter representation, local counterterm space, ghost number, power counting, and whether the regulator preserves BRST. Prove the Ward/Slavnov–Taylor identity order by order, not by appealing to the scalar causal-extension theorem.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-121-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-122-Local anomalies are classes in the relative local complex \(H^{1,4}(s\mid d_H)\), under a declared finite-jet algebra and locality hypothesis. The Wess–Zumino consistency equation \(s a_4+d_Ha_3=0\) is necessary; exactness means a local counterterm removes the breaking. Show that the relevant obstruction class vanishes for the specified matter representation and regulator before claiming a consistent gauge theory. A semisimple non-Abelian local anomaly, mixed \(U(1)\) anomalies, and global anomalies are distinct. The global \(SU(2)\) anomaly is not detected by a local polynomial in \(H^{1,4}(s\mid d_H)\). The anomaly-matching agent owns the physical representation/defect interface; this gauge scaffold should provide the local complex, not duplicate that output.
