+
+## Definition
+
+Assume the axiom of countable choice \(\mathrm{AC}_\omega\). Let \(M\) be a finite-dimensional, Hausdorff, second-countable smooth manifold without boundary. Fix a compact noncommutative finite-dimensional real Lie group \(G\), its Lie algebra \(\mathfrak g=T_eG\), a smooth right principal bundle \(\pi:P\to M\), and a principal connection form \(\omega\). Thus \(\omega\) is a smooth \(\mathfrak g\)-valued one-form that sends the fundamental vertical vector generated by \(Z\in\mathfrak g\) to \(Z\), has kernel the supplied horizontal distribution, and obeys \(R_g^*\omega=\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\omega\). In a local section \(s:U\to P\), the potential \(A=s^*\omega\) changes under a section change \(s'=su\), for smooth \(u:U\to G\), by \(A'=\operatorname{Ad}_{u^{-1}}A+\theta_L(u)\), where \(\theta_L(u)=u^*\theta_L\) is the pullback of the left Maurer–Cartan form. The full gauge group consists of smooth equivariant diffeomorphisms of \(P\) over \(\operatorname{id}_M\); this definition gives it no finite-dimensional Lie-group structure.
+
+A **finite-dimensional regulated gauge-slice datum** consists of integers \(1\le k\le n\); a finite-dimensional Lie group \(K\) of dimension \(k\) with a chosen smooth positive right-invariant density \(\mu_K\); a finite-dimensional smooth manifold \(X\) of dimension \(n\) with a chosen smooth positive density \(\mu_X\); and a smooth right action \(\alpha:X\times K\to X\), written \(x\cdot g\), with \((x\cdot g)\cdot h=x\cdot(gh)\). The group \(K\) and its action on \(X\) are separate regulator data. They are not identified with the full gauge group or the full space of connections unless a particular finite model explicitly constructs such an action and verifies that it preserves \(X\).
+
+Choose an embedded smooth submanifold \(S\subset X\) of codimension \(k\), a smooth positive density \(\mu_S\), and an open neighborhood \(U\subset X\) of a point \(s\in S\). A **gauge condition** on \(U\) is a smooth map \(\chi:U\to\mathbb R^k\) such that \(U\cap S=\chi^{-1}(0)\) and, for every \(s'\in U\cap S\), \(d\chi_{s'}\) is surjective with kernel \(T_{s'}S\). The **infinitesimal orbit map** at \(s'\) is \(R_{s'}:\mathfrak k\to T_{s'}X\), \(R_{s'}Z=\left.\frac{d}{dt}\right|_{t=0}s'\cdot\exp_K(tZ)\). Its kernel is the Lie algebra of the stabilizer of \(s'\). The **Faddeev–Popov operator** is the ordinary linear map \(M_{s'}:=d\chi_{s'}\circ R_{s'}:\mathfrak k\to\mathbb R^k\). It is **transverse at \(s'\)** when \(M_{s'}\) is an isomorphism, equivalently when \(T_{s'}X=T_{s'}S\oplus\operatorname{im}R_{s'}\). Choose a positive density \(\nu\) on \(\mathfrak k\) equal to \((\mu_K)_e\), and the standard positive density \(\lambda\) on \(\mathbb R^k\). The finite-dimensional Faddeev–Popov Jacobian \(\Delta_{\mathrm{FP}}(s')\) is the nonnegative density ratio defined by \(M_{s'}^*\lambda=\Delta_{\mathrm{FP}}(s')\nu\); in chosen bases it is \(|\det M_{s'}|\), and it is zero when \(M_{s'}\) is singular. A nonzero infinitesimal stabilizer therefore forces a zero Jacobian, while a discrete stabilizer can instead leave a separate finite intersection multiplicity.
+
+This datum defines only finite-dimensional Jacobians and local coordinate slices. It neither defines a determinant of an infinite-dimensional operator nor constructs a functional measure or a continuum path integral. A finite-mode or lattice application must supply the regulator manifold \(X\), the finite-dimensional group \(K\), their smooth action, and the domains and densities above.
+
+## Definition references
+
+[Skinner, finite-dimensional Faddeev–Popov model and its limitations](https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/dbs26/AQFT/chap8.pdf)
+
+[Mitter and Viallet, Sobolev gauge slices and local orbit-space geometry](https://scispace.com/pdf/on-the-bundle-of-connections-and-the-gauge-orbit-manifold-in-1nks9qpdkj.pdf)
+
