## Statement

Assume \(\mathrm{AC}_\omega\) and let \((X,K,\alpha,S,\chi,\mu_X,\mu_K,\mu_S)\) be finite-dimensional regulated gauge-slice data as defined above, with \(\dim X=n\), \(\dim K=k\), \(1\le k\le n\), and \(s_0\in S\cap U\). Thus \(K\) acts smoothly on the right, \(S\) is an embedded codimension-\(k\) submanifold, \(\chi:U\to\mathbb R^k\) is a smooth defining submersion with \(\ker d\chi_s=T_sS\), and the Faddeev–Popov operator is \(M_s=d\chi_s\circ R_s\). Suppose \(M_{s_0}\) is an isomorphism. For \(\Psi(g,s)=s\cdot g\), there are neighborhoods \(V\) of the identity in \(K\), \(W\) of \(s_0\) in \(S\), and \(O\) of \(s_0\) in \(X\) such that \(\Psi:V\times W\to O\) is a \(C^1\) diffeomorphism. Consequently, each orbit has exactly one representative in \(W\) among the representatives reached using \(V\).

Fix group coordinates \(\xi\) near the identity, slice coordinates \(z\) near \(s_0\), and adapted target coordinates \((y,z)=(\chi,z)\) near \(s_0\). Write the densities there as \(\mu_K=\rho_K(\xi)|d\xi|\), \(\mu_S=\rho_S(z)|dz|\), and \(\mu_X=\rho_X(y,z)|dy\,dz|\). The positive Jacobian \(J_\Psi\) defined by \(\Psi^*\mu_X=J_\Psi(\mu_K\otimes\mu_S)\) satisfies

$$
J_\Psi(e,s_0)=\frac{\rho_X(0,z_0)}{\rho_S(z_0)}\,\Delta_{\mathrm{FP}}(s_0),
$$

where \(\Delta_{\mathrm{FP}}\) uses the density \((\mu_K)_e\) and the standard density on \(\mathbb R^k\). Thus, when the target and slice densities are normalized so that \(\rho_X(0,z_0)=\rho_S(z_0)\), the ordinary finite-dimensional orbit-to-slice Jacobian is exactly \(\Delta_{\mathrm{FP}}(s_0)\).

For the exact multiplicity statement, fix \(x\in X\) and a relatively compact open set \(\Gamma\subset K\) such that \(x\cdot\overline{\Gamma}\subset U\). Let \(F_x(g)=\chi(x\cdot g)\). Suppose \(F_x\) has exactly \(r\) zeros \(g_1,\ldots,g_r\) in the support of a smooth function \(\varphi\) compactly supported in \(\Gamma\), each \(M_{x\cdot g_i}\) is invertible, and the slice points \(s_i=x\cdot g_i\) are distinct. Choose a nonnegative \(\eta\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb R^k)\) with integral \(1\), and set \(\eta_\epsilon(y)=\epsilon^{-k}\eta(y/\epsilon)\). Then

$$
\lim_{\epsilon\downarrow0}\int_\Gamma
\varphi(g)\,\eta_\epsilon(F_x(g))\,\Delta_{\mathrm{FP}}(x\cdot g)\,d\mu_K(g)
=\sum_{i=1}^r\varphi(g_i).
$$

In particular, if \(\varphi=1\) near the zeros, this finite-regulator expression counts the \(r\) distinct transverse intersections exactly. The theorem asserts only a local chart and this finite-dimensional multiplicity formula; it gives no global gauge section, functional determinant, or continuum path-integral measure.

## Statement references

[Skinner, finite-dimensional orbit-to-slice Jacobian and intersection multiplicity](https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/dbs26/AQFT/chap8.pdf)

[Mitter and Viallet, local Sobolev slice theorem and its free-action hypotheses](https://scispace.com/pdf/on-the-bundle-of-connections-and-the-gauge-orbit-manifold-in-1nks9qpdkj.pdf)

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** \(\mathrm{AC}_\omega\), the finite-dimensional regulated slice data, \(s_0\), the invertible map \(M_{s_0}\), and, for the multiplicity clause, \(x,\Gamma,\varphi,\eta\) and the stated finite zero set.

[A1] \(\mathrm{AC}_\omega\) is countable choice: every countable family of nonempty sets has a choice function. The qftx gauge-data chain inherits this assumption; no arbitrary-index choice is added here. [[def-countable-choice]]

[F1] [[def-qftx-gauge-slice-and-faddeev-popov-operator]]: the regulator supplies finite-dimensional \(X\), a smooth right \(K\)-action, an embedded slice \(S=\chi^{-1}(0)\), the orbit map \(R_s=d(\alpha_s)_e\), and \(M_s=d\chi_s\circ R_s\), with its determinant measured relative to the declared densities.

[F2] [[thm-euclidean-inverse-function-theorem]]: for a \(C^1\) map between open subsets of equal-dimensional Euclidean spaces, an invertible derivative at a point gives neighborhoods on which the map is a \(C^1\) diffeomorphism.

[F3] [[cor-change-of-variables-for-compactly-supported-functions]]: an injective \(C^1\) map between open subsets of \(\mathbb R^m\), with invertible derivative throughout, gives the compactly supported Riemann change-of-variables formula with factor \(|\det Dg|\).

## Proof

1.1 Choose a submanifold chart \((z,w)\) at \(s_0\) in which \(S\) is given by \(w=0\). Since \(\ker d\chi_{s_0}=T_{s_0}S\) and \(d\chi_{s_0}\) is onto, the \(w\)-derivative of \(\chi\) is invertible. The map \((z,w)\mapsto(\chi(z,w),z)\) therefore has invertible derivative; applying [F2] gives adapted target coordinates \((y,z)=(\chi,z)\) near \(s_0\). This argument uses only the supplied finite-dimensional data [F1] and the inherited countable-choice background [A1]. [given, A1, F1, F2, algebra]

2.1 In the source coordinates \((\xi,z)\) on \(K\times S\) and the adapted target coordinates \((y,z)\), the differential of \(\Psi(g,s)=s\cdot g\) at \((e,s_0)\) has block form

$$
D\Psi_{(e,s_0)}=
\begin{pmatrix}
M_{s_0} & 0\\
* & I_{n-k}
\end{pmatrix}.
$$

The upper-right block is zero because \(\chi=0\) on \(S\), and the lower-right block is the identity because \(z\) is the slice coordinate. Since \(M_{s_0}\) is invertible, \(D\Psi_{(e,s_0)}\) is invertible. Applying [F2] in these charts gives the required neighborhoods and local diffeomorphism. [given, F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra]

3.1 The same block determinant equals the coordinate determinant of \(M_{s_0}\). Dividing by the source density \(\rho_K(0)\rho_S(z_0)|d\xi\,dz|\) and using \(\Delta_{\mathrm{FP}}(s_0)=|\det M_{s_0}|/\rho_K(0)\) gives \(J_\Psi(e,s_0)=\rho_X(0,z_0)\Delta_{\mathrm{FP}}(s_0)/\rho_S(z_0)\). On neighborhoods from step 2.1, \(\Psi\) is injective \(C^1\) with invertible derivative. Applying [F3] in coordinates to any compactly supported Riemann-integrable \(f\) gives

$$
\int_O f\,d\mu_X
=\int_{V\times W}(f\circ\Psi)J_\Psi\,d\mu_K\,d\mu_S.
$$

Thus the Faddeev–Popov determinant is the transverse coordinate Jacobian with the displayed density normalization. [F1, F3, step 2.1, algebra]

4.1 At a zero \(g_i\) of \(F_x(g)=\chi(x\cdot g)\), use the left-invariant tangent frame \(Z\mapsto d(L_{g_i})_eZ\) on \(K\). The right-action identity \(x\cdot(g_i\exp_K(tZ))=(x\cdot g_i)\cdot\exp_K(tZ)\) shows that \(D(F_x)_{g_i}\), in this frame and the target density, is \(M_{s_i}\). Hence [F2] gives a local \(C^1\) inverse at each zero, and [F3] changes variables \(y=F_x(g)\) on each disjoint zero neighborhood:

$$
\int \varphi(g)\eta_\epsilon(F_x(g))\Delta_{\mathrm{FP}}(x\cdot g)\,d\mu_K(g)
=\int \varphi(F_{x,i}^{-1}(y))\eta_\epsilon(y)\,dy.
$$

For each \(i\), the right side tends to \(\varphi(g_i)\): its difference from \(\varphi(g_i)\) is bounded by \(\sup_{|y|\le C\epsilon}|\varphi(F_{x,i}^{-1}(y))-\varphi(g_i)|\int\eta_\epsilon(y)\,dy\), which tends to zero by continuity and the normalization \(\int\eta_\epsilon=1\). On the compact remainder of \(\operatorname{supp}\varphi\) there are no zeros, so \(|F_x|\) has a positive minimum there and the integrand vanishes for sufficiently small \(\epsilon\). Summing the \(r\) disjoint branches gives the stated exact orbit-intersection count. [F1, F2, F3, step 3.1, algebra] \(\;\;\Box\)

## Remarks

The diagram records the tangent-space calculation used in step 2.1. The upper arrow is the derivative of the local orbit-to-slice coordinate map; the lower arrow measures only its group-orbit component. The square commutes because \(d\chi_s\) vanishes on \(T_sS\). The proof above establishes invertibility and the Jacobian independently of the diagram.

~~~tikzcd
\mathfrak k\oplus T_sS \arrow[r,"D\Psi_{(e,s)}"] \arrow[d,"\operatorname{pr}_{\mathfrak k}"'] & T_sX \arrow[d,"d\chi_s"] \\
\mathfrak k \arrow[r,"M_s"'] & \mathbb R^k
~~~

